# Upstream refs this repo's CI builds its HIF dependencies from.
#
# These are passed straight to actions/checkout's `ref:`, which takes a branch
# name as readily as a commit SHA. They name `develop` deliberately: CI tracks
# the upstream development branches and there is nothing here to bump.
#
# This replaces per-commit pinning. The history above this line used to be a
# list of deliberate bumps, each one correct and each one hand-made; the reason
# it ends here is that the mechanism does not survive inattention. These pins
# had drifted 2, 10 and 22 commits behind hif-core, hif-frontend and
# hif-backend respectively before anyone noticed - so this repository's CI had
# been testing a toolchain nobody was running, which is the failure mode
# pinning was supposed to prevent.
#
# The trade was made knowingly, and both halves of it are real:
#
#   gained  - integration breakage surfaces the day it lands, and adopting an
#             upstream fix costs nothing.
#   lost    - a red check here no longer means "this diff broke something". It
#             can also mean an upstream commit landed. Before assuming your
#             change is at fault, look at the sibling repository's recent
#             history; the build log records which commit it checked out.
#
# This repository is the most exposed of the three, because it sits downstream
# of all of them: a defect in either the frontend or the backend reaches its
# fault-injection flow before anything else here can go wrong. That is a reason
# to track develop rather than an argument against it - it is where cross-repo
# breakage should be visible first.
#
# Reproducibility now lives where it belongs rather than here: git history for
# any past build, and hif-regression's manifests/stable.yaml for the released
# toolchain baseline.
HIF_CORE_REF=develop
HIF_FRONTEND_REF=develop
HIF_BACKEND_REF=develop
